1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,460
Twelve small payments. One collector. Look at each transfer alone,

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,880
and you miss the connection. Corridor
helps a payments analyst follow the wider

3
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:12,050
story, then explain what needs attention and why.

4
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:15,710
Three kinds of context live together in FalkorDB:

5
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:19,630
payment relationships, company knowledge, and previous investigations.

6
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:23,560
That means the analyst can follow the
money, find the relevant policy, and see

7
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:27,450
what the team already knows, without
piecing together separate conversations.

8
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:29,950
Let's start in the working app.

9
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:34,210
The demo uses realistic, fully synthetic payment scenarios.

10
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:38,920
Open an account's history to see earlier
decisions, or investigate directly from

11
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:42,670
the queue. Each new investigation keeps its own record.

12
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:47,430
Here, we're investigating the collector. This is a live run,

13
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:50,130
shortened for the demo. History comes first.

14
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:53,230
Then findings arrive as the agent checks connected accounts.

15
00:00:53,460 --> 00:00:55,430
It chooses from fixed graph tools;

16
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:59,840
it cannot invent a query. You can watch
the evidence build instead of waiting for

17
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:03,970
an unexplained answer. Select a finding and its accounts light up.

18
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:07,680
Switch to a flat view to read the
connections, or explore the graph in three

19
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:11,070
dimensions. The useful part is the link back to evidence:

20
00:01:11,540 --> 00:01:13,710
you can inspect the relationships behind a claim.

21
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:17,330
Sharing a device, on its own, is not proof of fraud.

22
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:20,810
The model recommends; code checks the recommendation.

23
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:24,720
A freeze requires two different positive
evidence tools to corroborate the same

24
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:28,330
account. Repeating a check doesn't count twice.

25
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:31,750
When support is insufficient, the case stays under review.

26
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:36,550
These are demo decisions: Corridor does not move or freeze real money.

27
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:39,130
A decision also needs an owner.

28
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:44,440
The saved case brings together its policy,
source document, current precedent, and

29
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:48,680
responsible employee. An analyst can
see the basis for the decision and who

30
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:52,370
should take it. The policies in this demonstration are fictional.

31
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:56,050
And when someone asks why, replay the investigation.

32
00:01:56,960 --> 00:02:00,940
Step through the findings and open the
underlying parameters, results, and fixed

33
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:03,890
query. That makes the conversation concrete:

34
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:09,100
which evidence supports this decision,
and what still needs a person's judgment?

35
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:12,210
Now open a different account: mule zero.

36
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:16,400
Its history lookup finds the earlier
collector investigation because both accounts

37
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:20,650
belong to that case. The previous case
identifier is right here in the result.

38
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:25,220
The next investigation starts with context
the team has already built, rather than

39
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:29,130
starting from scratch. Corridor brings connected evidence,

40
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:33,090
company context, and lasting case memory into one workspace.

41
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:37,340
Built for explaining decisions, reviewing
them, and carrying what you learn into

42
00:02:37,380 --> 00:02:42,130
the next case. Try the app, explore the
source, and follow the evidence yourself.
